WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.246
Many message contracts support several
known variants.

2
00:00:03.246 --> 00:00:05.443
A notification request might describe
email

3
00:00:05.443 --> 00:00:07.457
delivery or text message delivery.

4
00:00:07.457 --> 00:00:09.745
A discriminator identifies the variant,
and

5
00:00:09.745 --> 00:00:12.491
a runtime schema checks the corresponding
fields.

6
00:00:12.491 --> 00:00:15.512
The email variant requires its email
destination shape;

7
00:00:15.512 --> 00:00:18.990
the text message variant requires its
phone destination shape.

8
00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:21.919
Validating only that the discriminator is
a string

9
00:00:21.919 --> 00:00:24.665
leaves the rest of the contract
unresolved.

10
00:00:24.665 --> 00:00:27.228
Use a closed set of supported
discriminator

11
00:00:27.228 --> 00:00:29.974
values and validate the entire selected
branch.

12
00:00:29.974 --> 00:00:33.636
Decide how to handle fields belonging to
another branch and

13
00:00:33.636 --> 00:00:37.114
apply the same unknown property policy to
nested objects.

14
00:00:37.114 --> 00:00:39.768
After validation, a fixed switch or a

15
00:00:39.768 --> 00:00:42.515
server maintained mapping can select the
implementation.

16
00:00:42.515 --> 00:00:45.810
TypeScript's discriminated unions help the
compiler narrow each branch

17
00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:49.197
and identify missing cases, but that
assistance depends on

18
00:00:49.197 --> 00:00:52.675
the runtime value having already passed
the corresponding checks.

19
00:00:52.675 --> 00:00:56.519
Do not let a client supplied class name,
module path,

20
00:00:56.519 --> 00:01:00.089
constructor name, or function name choose
arbitrary application behavior.

21
00:01:00.089 --> 00:01:03.018
Reflective object construction and
automatic hydration can expose

22
00:01:03.018 --> 00:01:06.131
operations that were never intended as
public input.

23
00:01:06.131 --> 00:01:08.693
Richer serialization formats that
reconstruct object graphs

24
00:01:08.693 --> 00:01:11.440
or special executable types require
particular scrutiny.

25
00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:15.192
For ordinary application requests, prefer
data values that describe an

26
00:01:15.192 --> 00:01:18.762
allowed operation, followed by explicit
code that performs it.

27
00:01:18.762 --> 00:01:22.607
Construct domain objects under server
control after the necessary checks.

28
00:01:22.607 --> 00:01:24.987
The discriminator itself conveys no
authority.

29
00:01:24.987 --> 00:01:27.550
Selecting an email variant does not
authorize

30
00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:30.570
contacting every destination, and
selecting a recognized operation

31
00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:33.316
does not establish permission to perform
it.

32
00:01:33.316 --> 00:01:36.245
Apply authorization and semantic rules
after the branch

33
00:01:36.245 --> 00:01:38.992
is understood and before its effects
occur.

34
00:01:38.992 --> 00:01:41.646
Versioned messages need the same
discipline: accept

35
00:01:41.646 --> 00:01:44.392
explicitly supported versions, validate
their specific contracts,

36
00:01:44.392 --> 00:01:47.138
and map them into the internal
representation.

37
00:01:47.138 --> 00:01:49.518
Reject unknown variants and versions
predictably.

38
00:01:49.518 --> 00:01:52.447
Avoid a fallback that attempts arbitrary
loading or

39
00:01:52.447 --> 00:01:55.468
loosely accepts a future shape, because
that transfers

40
00:01:55.468 --> 00:01:58.580
control of the application's behavior to
unverified data.

